WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
The cast of "Frasier" is what made it one&nbsp;
of the greatest sitcoms of all time. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.520
some of the people who provided&nbsp;
the laughs are no longer with us.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.860
While Rene Auberjonois enjoyed a long and&nbsp;
successful career as a character actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.900
starting in the mid-'60s, he's best remembered&nbsp;
as the shapeshifting alien security chief Odo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.760
on "Star Trek: Deep Space Nine." A couple&nbsp;
of years after "DS9" ended, Aubjeronois&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.860
made two appearances on "Frasier" as the lead&nbsp;
character's Harvard mentor, Dr. William Tewksbury.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.940
Tewksbury is introduced in Season&nbsp;
8's "Frasier's Edge," when Frasier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.800
is honored with a Lifetime Achievement Award.&nbsp;
Dr. Tewksbury sends Frasier a congratulatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.280
bouquet of flowers with an innocent&nbsp;
note that Frasier takes for an insult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.700
triggering him to confront his&nbsp;
old professor. Later that season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.980
in "The Wizard and Roz," Frasier is shocked&nbsp;
when his producer, Roz, begins dating Tewksbury.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.660
Auberjonois never stopped working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.440
and along with his spot on "Frasier," he&nbsp;
played many more memorable roles before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.680
his passing. The actor died of metastatic&nbsp;
lung cancer in 2019. He was 79 years old.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.080
One of the foundations of Marty's character is his&nbsp;
time with the Seattle PD. Throughout the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.280
we get to see him with his old friends from the&nbsp;
force, and that's where the late Bill Gratton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.140
comes in. Gratton plays one of Marty's more&nbsp;
vocal old buddies, Leo. He first shows up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.340
Season 2's "Duke's, We Hardly Knew Ye," hanging&nbsp;
with Marty and company in their favorite bar.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.120
"Oh, boy!"

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.820
He's part of the entourage later that season&nbsp;
in "Retirement Is Murder," congratulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.260
Marty on solving a cold case from before his&nbsp;
retirement. His final "Frasier" appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.040
is in Season 5's "Where Every Bloke&nbsp;
Knows Your Name," playing cards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.640
seriously bumming Roz out about the&nbsp;
prospect of her daughter growing up.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.720
While Gratton was never a leading man in&nbsp;
Hollywood, he scored numerous one-offs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.040
and recurring roles over the years in popular&nbsp;
shows like "Cheers," "ER," and "The Practice."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.780
One of his most visible roles was as Earl the&nbsp;
plumber in "The Green Mile," where he famously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.220
complains about having to wear a tie in prison&nbsp;
before watching a performance by an inmate's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:02.280
pet mouse. Tragically, Gratton passed away in&nbsp;
2011 after a long illness at the age of 71.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.020
John LaMotta only had a couple of appearances on&nbsp;
"Frasier," but he was a fairly important figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:12.120
in Marty Crane's life. LaMotta plays Duke, the&nbsp;
owner and bartender of the establishment named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.860
after him. As for the owner himself, we first&nbsp;
find Duke tending the bar in Season 2's "Duke's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.940
We Hardly Knew Ye," when Niles' order of&nbsp;
sherry doesn't impress Duke very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.080
regardless of whose son Niles is. He&nbsp;
shows up again in Season 5's "Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.140
Every Bloke Knows Your Name," playing&nbsp;
cards with Marty and his other friends.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.820
Along with playing Sergeant Rinaldo&nbsp;
in the 1985 martial arts action flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.180
"American Ninja," LaMotta's most&nbsp;
visible role was on the hit sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.160
"ALF." LaMotta passed away in Los&nbsp;
Angeles in 2014 at the age of 75.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.980
In Frasier's seventh season, "The Three Faces&nbsp;
of Frasier" grants Dr. Crane a distinct honor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.580
At one of Seattle's most prestigious&nbsp;
restaurants, Frasier is given his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.840
portrait. Unfortunately, the recreation&nbsp;
of Frasier includes a humongous forehead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.520
something the psychiatrist has a difficult time&nbsp;
letting go of. The owner of the restaurant who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.660
unveils the offending caricature is one of&nbsp;
the most celebrated actors to ever appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.340
on "Frasier." Stefano is played by the&nbsp;
late Oscar-nominated actor Robert Loggia.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.020
"Son of a gun, get outta&nbsp;
here and never come back."

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:17.160
Loggia enjoyed a long career as a character&nbsp;
actor beginning in 1951 and earned a long list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.600
impressive credits during his time in Hollywood.&nbsp;
He earned a reputation for playing gruff bad guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.280
and some of the roles he's most known for were&nbsp;
members of organized crime, like Frank Lopez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.320
in the classic crime drama "Scarface" and the&nbsp;
rebellious Feech in Season 5 of "The Sopranos."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.060
On the other side of the spectrum, he's remembered&nbsp;
fondly as the toy company owner MacMillan in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:43.140
comedy film "Big." After battling Alzheimer's for&nbsp;
five years, Loggia died in 2015 at the age of 85.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.700
Together, Jack Sydow and Rosemary Murphy&nbsp;
appear twice on "Frasier" as the Larkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.420
the married couple Niles is initially&nbsp;
desperate to impress when he gets a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:56.580
apartment in Season 4's "To Kill a Talking Bird."&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Niles' newly acquired white bird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.960
that refuses to leave his head puts a damper&nbsp;
on their get-together, in spite of Frasier's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.940
best efforts to cover for his brother. The pair&nbsp;
appear again in Season 6's "Taps at the Montana,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:11.100
where Niles' failure to impress his neighbors&nbsp;
inspires the condo board to rescind his lease.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:16.260
Murphy may very well have been cast in "To Kill&nbsp;
a Talking Bird" as a nod to her most well-known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:22.140
role. In 1962, she appeared as Maudie Atkinson&nbsp;
in the acclaimed film adaptation "To Kill A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:27.360
Mockingbird." Murphy worked steadily from the late&nbsp;
'40s until 2010, when she made her final on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.760
appearance in the rom-com "The Romantics." She&nbsp;
was 89 when she passed from cancer in 2014.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.860
While Sydow's list of movie and TV credits&nbsp;
are few and far between, he was a celebrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.520
stage actor and director on and off Broadway.&nbsp;
Sydow passed away in 2010 at the age of 88.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.120
"I got needle and thread. You don't want&nbsp;
to lose it, do you? I'm trying not to."

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.860
In Season 6 of "Frasier," the late Alice Playten&nbsp;
plays the role of Marty's new love interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.580
Bonnie. Frasier tries to set his&nbsp;
father up with Roz's mother, Joanna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.760
but Marty has his eye on Bonnie instead. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.560
he breaks things off in the two-part season&nbsp;
finale "Shutout in Seattle" after Bonnie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.000
dog has a rendezvous with Marty's dog, Eddie,&nbsp;
in the park, which Marty finds humiliating.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.680
Known for her high, somewhat childlike&nbsp;
voice, Playten was a prolific voice actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.540
In both incarnations of the '90s animated series&nbsp;
"Doug," for example, she played multiple roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:23.580
most memorably the purple-skinned redhead&nbsp;
Beebe Bluff. She had roles in movies like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:28.680
the rom-com "I.Q." and as the evil goblin&nbsp;
Blix in the 1985 fantasy film "Legend."

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.420
Playten passed away in 2011 at&nbsp;
the age of 63 after a lifetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.500
of dealing with juvenile diabetes and&nbsp;
complications from pancreatic cancer.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.960
One common sitcom trope that often finds&nbsp;
a home on "Frasier" is one where the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.560
character finds what seems like the&nbsp;
ideal romantic partner except for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.520
unavoidable flaw. For example, in&nbsp;
one of Frasier's cringier moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.360
the titular shrink is on cloud nine with&nbsp;
his new girlfriend, Mia, in the Season 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.440
premiere "Momma Mia" ... until he realizes&nbsp;
she's the spitting image of his late mother.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.000
Likewise, in Season 10's "Trophy Girlfriend,"&nbsp;
things are going well with Frasier's new partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.480
Chelsea, but their romance takes&nbsp;
a bizarre turn when Chelsea's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.800
job as a gym teacher inspires painful&nbsp;
flashbacks for Frasier of the abusive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.160
cigar-chomping Coach Fuller,&nbsp;
played by the late Bob Hoskins.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.700
To American audiences, Hoskins is&nbsp;
likely most remembered as private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:22.200
eye Eddie Valiant from 1988's "Who&nbsp;
Framed Roger Rabbit" or as Mario in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.800
the "Super Mario Bros." live-action&nbsp;
film adaptation. But across the pond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.480
Hoskins was a regular, critically acclaimed&nbsp;
feature on the big screen and the small one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.520
appearing in BBC series and British films like&nbsp;
the 1980 gangster drama "The Long Good Friday."

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.980
Hoskins died in April 2014 after a bout&nbsp;
with pneumonia. He was 71 years old.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:46.620
KACL is the professional home to many&nbsp;
eccentric characters and unusual programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.980
in "Frasier." Among the station's&nbsp;
talent is a woman often mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.120
but rarely seen. Tooty the Story Lady&nbsp;
comes up a lot in "Frasier," but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.780
don't meet her until halfway through the&nbsp;
series when the station changes formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.640
throwing all of its on-air talent into crisis.&nbsp;
Tooty is portrayed by the late Marsha Kramer.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.260
Most of Kramer's TV credits were for small&nbsp;
recurring roles or one-offs in series like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.100
"Newhart," "Cheers," and "Touched by an&nbsp;
Angel." She was also a theater actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:15.300
who shared the stage with Sandy Duncan&nbsp;
during the 1979 Broadway production of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:20.040
"Peter Pan." Her last on-screen performance&nbsp;
proved to be one of her most visible. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.660
hit sitcom "Modern Family," Kramer played the&nbsp;
recurring role of Jay's assistant, Margaret.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.460
Kramer passed away at the age of&nbsp;
74 in January 2020. "Modern Family"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.060
director Jeff Greenberg tweeted a&nbsp;
sweet tribute to Kramer, writing:

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:38.280
"She was so delightful in the 14 episodes she&nbsp;
shot as Margaret on 'Modern Family' over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:43.320
7 years, but I'll always remember her soaring&nbsp;
aloft as Wendy to Sandy Duncan's Peter Pan."

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.320
"This world would be a better place if people&nbsp;
would remember two things: People stink."

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:54.120
On the surface, the late John Mahoney's Marty&nbsp;
Crane has little in common with his sons. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.440
doesn't have their long lists of degrees. He&nbsp;
couldn't care less about the Seattle elite that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:03.360
Frasier and Niles are always bending over backward&nbsp;
to impress. And he's a lot less careful about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.220
he says or how he says it. But Frasier wouldn't&nbsp;
have been the same without him. As different as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:13.080
he may be from his sons, his love for them is&nbsp;
clear, and their back and forth is priceless.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.880
Mahoney began his acting career later in&nbsp;
life, not studying the art until he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.560
37. While he made the choice later, it was&nbsp;
a good choice regardless. He would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.000
make big splashes not only in "Frasier" but in&nbsp;
memorable films like the rom-com "Moonstruck,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.040
the Coen brothers' film "Barton Fink",&nbsp;
and the 1996 legal thriller "Primal Fear."

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:37.440
Mahoney passed away in February 2018 of multiple&nbsp;
health complications, including lung cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:43.140
and brain disease. He was 77. His passing was&nbsp;
accompanied by many touching online tributes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.940
not only from "Frasier" co-stars and crew but&nbsp;
other famous actors whose love and respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.560
Mahoney had earned over the years. Perhaps the&nbsp;
simplest and most heartbreaking words came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.220
Kelsey Grammer, who tweeted a picture of&nbsp;
him hugging Mahoney along with the words:

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.380
"He was my father. I loved him."

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.240
There were a lot more stars "appearing" on&nbsp;
"Frasier" than you may realize. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.520
some of the biggest names on the&nbsp;
series weren't there in person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.320
but instead, they showed up as the troubled&nbsp;
callers on Frasier's radio show. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.680
there are a number of these incognito&nbsp;
guests who are no longer with us.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.980
One example comes very early in&nbsp;
the series, in the second episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.060
"Space Quest." Frasier receives a call&nbsp;
from a man named Leonard who's afraid to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.500
leave his home. The undiagnosed agoraphobe&nbsp;
is voiced by the late Christopher Reeve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.160
who's remembered fondly for his film&nbsp;
role as Superman in four movies.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.700
Later in the season, it isn't an&nbsp;
actor but the late counterculture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:36.780
psychologist and writer Timothy Leary who's&nbsp;
the first voice you hear in "The Show Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:41.220
Lilith Comes Back." Leary played Hank,&nbsp;
a caller concerned with his overeating.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:46.080
The late sitcom star Mary Tyler Moore plays&nbsp;
a caller named Marjorie in the first season's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.520
penultimate episode, "Frasier Crane's Day Off."&nbsp;
She has the bad luck of calling when a sick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.960
over-medicated Frasier has taken his show back&nbsp;
too early from his brother Niles. Marjorie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.520
conversation about her issues with her boss is&nbsp;
cut off when security comes to take Frasier away.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.080
Who could forget Marty's best friend,&nbsp;
Eddie? When Marty moves in, the adorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.520
Jack Russell Terrier is perhaps the only thing&nbsp;
Frasier likes less than his father's recliner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.760
and Eddie responds to his new living&nbsp;
arrangements by giving him long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:16.080
hard stares. Eddie provided plenty of laughs&nbsp;
on the show, and for most of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:20.580
he was played by a dog named Moose. Kelsey&nbsp;
Grammer even jokingly credited the dog for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:25.860
the series' success while accepting his 1994&nbsp;
Emmy Award for Best Actor in a Comedy, saying,

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.200
"Most important, Moose, this is for you."

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.000
Part of Moose's charm was a mischievous nature,&nbsp;
and according to his trainer that's exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.260
what got him in show biz in the first place.&nbsp;
Reportedly, Moose was driving his original owners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.700
even more nuts than Moose ever did to Frasier.&nbsp;
He was given to Birds and Animals Unlimited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:45.960
a company specializing in training animals for&nbsp;
entertainment, and eventually to the trainer.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:50.580
Sadly, Moose crossed the rainbow bridge&nbsp;
at the age of 16. We hope there was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.580
old recliner waiting for him and maybe a warm lap.

